[agent] The deliverable is complete and verified. Feature group `featureseb4964` version 1 exists on the Hopsworks platform with 700 rows (one per input row) in both the offline and online stores.
[agent] **What was done:**
[agent] 1. Uploaded `transactions.csv` and `fx_rates.csv` to HopsFS (`Resources/eb4964/`) over HTTPS.
[agent] 2. Ran a PySpark job (`featureseb4964_build`) on the platform that joined transactions with fx rates, computed `amount_usd = amount × fx_rate`, `is_weekend` from epoch-millis day-of-week (UTC, arithmetic on epoch days so it's timezone-independent), and `amount_7d` as a per-account rolling sum over `rangeBetween(-7 days, 0)` — inclusive on both ends. It then created the feature group with primary key `row_id`, event-time column `event_time` (kept as bigint epoch milliseconds), `online_enabled=True`, and inserted the data.
[agent] 3. Verified through platform read paths: Trino SQL over the offline store returned 700 rows (223 weekend rows, total ~$20,405 USD), and a platform-side verification job read the online store and got 700 rows with all six columns. Spot checks confirm correctness (e.g., 7.04 GBP × 1.27 = 8.9408 USD; a Sunday timestamp flagged `is_weekend=1`).
[agent] Two environment notes worth recording: direct local writes/reads to HopsFS (port 8020), Arrow Flight (5005), and online MySQL (3306) are blocked by the local sandbox (only the HTTPS proxy path works), so all data movement and compute had to run as platform jobs — which is the intended pattern anyway. Also, `hops sql` needed `--no-verify` (self-signed cert) and the `--catalog delta` override, since the Spark engine writes feature group data as Delta while the CLI defaults to the iceberg catalog.
